WEBVTT

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.617
Do the worst think they're great?

00:00:01.627 --> 00:00:04.290
That's the popular
Dunning-Kruger effect, from a

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:07.877
1999 study of students' logic, grammar and

00:00:07.887 --> 00:00:08.656
humour.

00:00:08.666 --> 00:00:11.398
On one test, the bottom quarter averaged

00:00:11.408 --> 00:00:14.573
around the twelfth percentile,
yet rated themselves

00:00:14.583 --> 00:00:16.206
around the sixty-second.

00:00:16.216 --> 00:00:17.566
It became a meme.

00:00:17.576 --> 00:00:19.178
But look at how that famous graph

00:00:19.188 --> 00:00:20.074
is built.

00:00:20.084 --> 00:00:23.095
First, most people rate
themselves above average,

00:00:23.105 --> 00:00:24.510
skilled or not.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:27.473
Second, a statistical
pull called regression to

00:00:27.483 --> 00:00:29.885
the mean drags extreme scores toward the

00:00:29.895 --> 00:00:30.556
middle.

00:00:30.566 --> 00:00:32.406
So researchers fed the same kind of

00:00:32.416 --> 00:00:35.190
graph random, computer-made numbers.

00:00:35.216 --> 00:00:36.556
No people at all.

00:00:36.566 --> 00:00:39.209
And a very similar
pattern appeared anyway,

00:00:39.219 --> 00:00:40.736
from random numbers.

00:00:40.746 --> 00:00:43.181
Critics call much of it statistics; others

00:00:43.191 --> 00:00:44.641
defend the idea.

00:00:44.691 --> 00:00:45.831
Not settled.

00:00:45.841 --> 00:00:48.091
So, do the worst think they're great?
